  "query_preview": "..\n\nCenter of high pressure shifts into the eastern Great \nLakes/Appalachians today allowing light southeasterly winds to \ndevelop locally. While this doesn't draw in much warm advection, it \ndoes shunt the coolest air out of the region supporting more uniform \nupper 50-lower 60 highs across SE MI. Cloudier skies today are the \nresult of a warm front lifting out of the Mississippi valley into \nGreat Lakes/upper Midwest. Primary theta-e axis is directed west of \nLake Michigan leading to the majority of associated showers holding \nwell to our west. That said, lower MI holds on the periphery of \nelevated moisture transport this afternoon into tonight allowing \nisolated to scattered light showers to work across the state. These \nwill struggle to survive into our CWA owing to the dry resident \nairmass with main chances (and low end at that) confined towards the \ncentral portion of the state and near the Ohio border. Any shower \nthat does get into the area will be brief and light. \n\nBroad Pacific Northwest trough slowly traverses the central CONUS \nnext week eventually bringing a return to active weather locally. \nTiming details remain nebulous given a good spread in the speed of \nthe longwave and if a northern stream Canadian shortwave can phase \nwith the trough. While rain could reach the southern lower MI as \nearly as Monday, the mid week period remains the favored window for \nwider spread showers with milder/humid air (by fall standards) in \nplace. The attendant cold front eventually crosses latter half of \nthe week bringing at least a brief return towards more average late \nfall temperatures though still likely several degrees above normal.",
